function chapterRoute(u,i){if(u.n===4&&i<10)return ["#unit4-design","#unit4-sql","#unit4-project","#unit4-analysis","#unit4-controls","#unit4-crypto","#unit4-ethics","#unit4-review","#unit4-consolidation","#unit4-exam"][i];if(u.n===3&&i<14)return ["#unit3-fluency","#unit3-structures","#unit3-search","#unit3-sort","#unit3-objects","#unit3-inheritance","#unit3-project","#unit3-reliability","#unit3-network-models","#unit3-addressing","#unit3-secure-networks","#unit3-review","#unit3-consolidation","#unit3-exam"][i];if(u.n===2&&i<10)return '#'+['database-foundations','entities-relationships','normalisation','sql-basics','database-solution','security-threats','responsible-data','unit2-review','unit2-consolidation','unit2-exam'][i];return u.n===1&&i<16?'#'+['start','values','representation','selection','loops','functions','arrays','files','design','software-responsibility','networks','addressing','network-design','network-review','consolidation','exam-preparation'][i]:`#plan-${u.n}-${i}`}
function cards(){return '<div class="grid">'+units.map(u=>`<section class="card" id="unit${u.n}"><span class="eyebrow">YEAR ${u.year} / UNIT ${u.n}</span><h2>${u.title}</h2><p>${u.desc}</p>${u.topics.map((t,i)=>`<a class="chapter" href="${chapterRoute(u,i)}">${String(i+1).padStart(2,'0')} &nbsp; ${t}<small>${(u.n===4&&i<10)||(u.n===1&&i<16)||(u.n===2&&i<10)||(u.n===3&&i<14)?'Draft':'Planned'}</small></a>`).join('')}</section>`).join('')+'</div>'}
function selection(){return `<article class="lesson"><p class="eyebrow">UNIT 1 / PROGRAMMING / WEEK 3 / CHAPTER 4</p><h1>Making decisions<br>with selection.</h1><p class="lede">Programs become useful when they can respond differently to different inputs. A condition tells a program which path to take.</p><span class="pill">PYTHON 3</span> <span class="pill">DRAFT · TEACHER REVIEW PENDING</span><div class="no-print" style="margin-top:20px"><button id="print">Print / save as PDF</button> <button id="download">Download Python example</button></div><h2>What you’ll learn</h2><div class="objectives"><ul><li>Explain how selection differs from sequence.</li><li>Use a comparison to produce a Boolean result.</li><li>Write an <code>if / else</code> decision in Python.</li><li>Test a decision just below, at and above its boundary.</li></ul></div><h2>1. From an instruction to a decision</h2><p>Sequence means following instructions in order. Selection introduces a choice. Imagine a school club that offers free delivery for orders worth at least $50. Smaller orders attract a $5 delivery charge.</p><p>The decision is: <em>is the order total greater than or equal to 50?</em> If the answer is true, delivery costs zero. Otherwise, delivery costs five dollars. Only one of these two branches runs.</p><div class="steps" aria-label="Decision process"><span>Read the order total</span><span>Test total ≥ 50</span><span>Choose the delivery charge</span></div><h2>2. Design before coding</h2><p>This illustrative pseudocode expresses the logic without Python syntax:</p><pre>INPUT order_total\nIF order_total &gt;= 50 THEN\n    delivery_charge = 0\nELSE\n    delivery_charge = 5\nEND IF\nOUTPUT delivery_charge</pre><p>Before implementation, define the input assumptions: the order total is a non-negative numeric amount. A complete application would also handle invalid input. This example focuses on the decision itself.</p><h2>3. Implement the decision in Python</h2><pre><code>order_total = float(input("Order total ($): "))\n\nif order_total &gt;= 50:\n    delivery_charge = 0\nelse:\n    delivery_charge = 5\n\nprint(f"Delivery charge: &#36;{delivery_charge:.2f}")</code></pre><p><code>&gt;=</code> compares two values and returns a Boolean. The colon introduces the branch’s body; indentation groups its statements. The final <code>print</code> is outside both branches, so it runs whichever branch was chosen.</p><div class="notice"><strong>Watch the boundary.</strong> “At least $50” includes exactly $50. Using <code>&gt;</code> would incorrectly charge delivery on a $50 order. Also, <code>=</code> assigns a value; <code>==</code> compares values.</div><h2>Combine conditions and choices</h2><p>Use <code>and</code> when both conditions must hold, <code>or</code> when either can hold, and <code>not</code> to reverse a Boolean. Python evaluates <code>not</code> before <code>and</code>, then <code>or</code>; parentheses can make your intended grouping clear. An eligibility rule might be <code>age &gt;= 16 and has_permission</code>.</p><pre>if score &gt;= 80:\n    band = "High"\nelif score &gt;= 50:\n    band = "Pass"\nelse:\n    band = "Developing"</pre><p>This chain runs the branch for the first condition that evaluates to true, or the else branch if no condition is true. Test scores 49, 50, 79 and 80: the expected bands are Developing, Pass, Pass and High. Checking score &gt;= 50 before score &gt;= 80 in the same if/elif chain would prevent a score of 80 from reaching the High branch.</p><h2>4. Trace it yourself</h2><p>Change the input below to explore this fixed example. This visualiser traces the decision; it does not execute arbitrary Python.</p><div class="card no-print"><label for="total">Order total ($, non-negative)</label><input id="total" type="number" min="0" step="0.01" value="50"><p id="trace" aria-live="polite"></p></div><h2>5. Test the boundary</h2><p>Choose tests that exercise both branches, with special attention to the point where the result changes.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Order total</th><th>Condition result</th><th>Expected charge</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>$49.99</td><td>False</td><td>$5.00</td></tr><tr><td>$50.00</td><td>True</td><td>$0.00</td></tr><tr><td>$50.01</td><td>True</td><td>$0.00</td></tr></tbody></table><p>These tests distinguish <code>&gt;=</code> from the common mistake <code>&gt;</code>. Test typical amounts such as $10 and $100 as well. Testing invalid text or negative totals belongs to input validation and is an extension to this example.</p><h2>6. Practise</h2><details><summary>Predict: what happens for an order worth $35?</summary><p><code>35 &gt;= 50</code> is false, so the else branch sets the charge to 5. The output is <code>Delivery charge: $5.00</code>.</p></details><details><summary>Debug: a $50 order is charged delivery. What might be wrong?</summary><p>The comparison may be <code>order_total &gt; 50</code>. Change it to <code>order_total &gt;= 50</code> and run the three boundary tests again.</p></details><details><summary>Apply: a museum charges $8 for ages under 16 and $12 otherwise. Design and test the decision.</summary><pre>if age &lt; 16:\n    ticket_price = 8\nelse:\n    ticket_price = 12</pre><p>Test ages 15, 16 and 17. Expected prices are $8, $12 and $12 respectively. Assume age is a valid non-negative integer.</p></details><div class="card"><h3>Quick check</h3><p>Which comparison correctly represents “at least 50”?</p><div id="quiz"><button data-answer="wrong">total &gt; 50</button> <button data-answer="right">total &gt;= 50</button> <button data-answer="wrong">total == 50</button></div><p id="quiz-feedback" class="feedback" aria-live="polite"></p></div><h2>Chapter recap</h2><p>Selection chooses a branch using a condition. Python uses indentation to group the instructions in each branch. Translate the requirement precisely, then test both paths and the decision boundary.</p><details><summary>Teaching notes & syllabus reference</summary><p>This draft addresses part of Unit 1 programming: selection, comparisons and boundary testing. The separate loops chapter extends this lesson into iteration. Suggested lesson: predict the result, trace three inputs, implement the example, then change the threshold and test again.</p><p>Consult the current <a href="${source}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SCSA syllabus and additional support booklet</a> for course requirements and pseudocode conventions. Review the example and terminology before classroom use.</p></details></article>`}
